<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Who needs 12 testers for 14 days?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For personal Google Play developer accounts created after November 13, 2023, at least 12 testers must stay opted in continuously for the 14 days before you apply. Once that condition is met, you can request production access in Play Console. Google reviews the application."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does mutual testing differ from private testing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In a free exchange, developers use each other’s builds and check in daily. With private testing, DevTestHub coordinates 12 or more testers on real Android devices. You follow the work without taking on partner apps."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why plan for 14+ days?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The minimum opt-in period is continuous. We usually schedule a full 15 days for a managed test, allowing a buffer for time-zone boundaries and Play Console updates. In an exchange, keep each partner’s agreed end date."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does the $75 review add to the basic test?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The basic test covers team activity and help answering the production-access questionnaire. It costs $20 with crypto or $23 with PayPal or bank transfer. The $75 total includes a written app review with device coverage, reproducible issues and a prioritized fix plan. Arrange it with the team in Telegram."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I connect testers to my build?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Add google-play-dev-test@googlegroups.com to the tester groups for your closed track. Make the build available in the required regions, send the changes for review and share the Android testing link. You keep sole control of your Play Console."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can the team test a paid app?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Add the tester group in Play Console’s License testing settings as well as in the closed track. Keep the license response at RESPOND_NORMALLY. The setup guide shows both places."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What if Google asks for more testing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Send us the review response. We continue testing until you receive production access or refund the service fee. You handle any code or policy changes Google requests. Google makes the final access decision."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does the bot use Telegram Stars?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Telegram requires Stars for digital services sold inside bots and Mini Apps. Choose Stars there, or use crypto, PayPal or bank transfer on this website. You can attach a receipt and follow the order in your account."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I join with an app that is already published?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. List the app in the Mini App and choose a task for other developers to try. They can assess clarity, ease of use and stability, document a bug or suggest a change. Public store reviews are always voluntary."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does a growth coordinator take on?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The coordinator organizes the team, manages reciprocal tests and follows up on participation. You set the product question, watch activity in the bot and compare the outcome with your Play Console data. Discuss scope and terms before handing over coordination."}}]}</script>
